    "body": "If you’re getting the ImageData object from a canvas context’s `getImageData`, you probably want to be using `canvas.toBlob()`. The Blob you get back can be represented as an “object URL,” a special local serialized pointer to the resource. This can be used as an img’s src.\r\n\r\nWhile there are probably some conversion paths that could get you from ImageData to ObjectURL, I’m guessing they’d be pretty roundabout. ImageData is a wrapper for raw bitmap data with associated dimensions (and colorspace). It’s not a specific encoded media type of the sort you’d normally want like image/png (which is the default for toBlob, but it’s configurable).\r\n\r\nThere are various ways to obtain blobs/files (File extends Blob), so maybe another strategy would more appropriate here. You mentioned perf as the motivation for the question, but I don’t think there’s enough context to know if this would or wouldn’t be any faster in practice than what you’re doing now. There are APIs optimized for particular image-rendering use cases, though, like OffscreenCanvas and ImageBitmapRenderingContext. Whether these would be applicable for what you’re doing isn’t clear.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe following demonstrates the premise of object URLs and may help you explore the API, but it isn’t intended as a literal suggestion. You wouldn’t actually hardcode the data this way, it’s just to give you something to touch/see without needing every duck in a row first.\r\n\r\n```js\r\nimg.src = URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([ Uint8Array.of(\r\n  // This is just for demonstration, you would be getting your blob / its data\r\n  // some other way.\r\n  0x89, 0x50, 0x4E, 0x47, 0x0D, 0x0A, 0x1A, 0x0A, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x0D, 0x49,\r\n  0x48, 0x44, 0x52, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x05, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x05, 0x08, 0x06,\r\n  0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x8D, 0x6F, 0x26, 0xE5, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x1C, 0x49, 0x44,\r\n  0x41, 0x54, 0x08, 0xD7, 0x63, 0xF8, 0xFF, 0xFF, 0x3F, 0xC3, 0x7F, 0x06, 0x20,\r\n  0x05, 0xC3, 0x20, 0x12, 0x84, 0xD0, 0x31, 0xF1, 0x82, 0x58, 0xCD, 0x04, 0x00,\r\n  0x0E, 0xF5, 0x35, 0xCB, 0xD1, 0x8E, 0x0E, 0x1F, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x49,\r\n  0x45, 0x4E, 0x44, 0xAE, 0x42, 0x60, 0x82\r\n) ], { type: \"image/png\" }));\r\n```\r\n\r\nI’m not an expert on these APIs or anything, so def don’t consider anything I said to be authoritative. In the future, I would suggest bringing questions of this sort to another venue, like stackoverflow, that’s intended for this sort of “how do I...” question. ",
